{"element":{"atomicNumber":45,"symbol":"Rh","name":"Rhodium","wikidataId":"Q1087","wikipediaTitle":"Rhodium","wikipediaUrl":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodium","period":5,"group":9,"block":"d","category":"transition metal","phase":"solid","mass":102.9055,"electronegativity":2.28,"ionizationEnergy":7.459,"radius":195,"summary":"Rhodium is a chemical element; it has symbol Rh and atomic number 45. It is a very rare, dark silvery-white, hard, corrosion-resistant transition metal. It is a noble metal and a member of the platinum group. It has only one naturally occurring isotope, which is 103Rh. Naturally occurring rhodium is usually found as a free metal or as an alloy with similar metals and rarely as a chemical compound in minerals such as bowieite and rhodplumsite. It is one of the rarest and most valuable precious metals. Rhodium is a group 9 element. It is used for the enhancement of jewelry.","neighbors":["Ru","Pd"],"rankings":{"mass":74,"electronegativity":15,"ionizationEnergy":44,"radius":66,"density":17,"meltingPoint":13,"boilingPoint":18,"halfLife":0},"sources":{"structured":{"provider":"PubChem","license":"public domain","url":"https://pubchem.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/element/45"},"identifiers":{"provider":"Wikidata","license":"CC0 1.0","url":"https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q1087"},"summary":{"provider":"Wikipedia","title":"Rhodium","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rhodium","license":"CC BY-SA 4.0","accessDate":"2026-03-30"}},"discoveryYear":1803,"discoverer":"William Hyde Wollaston","etymologyOrigin":"property","etymologyDescription":"Greek rhodon: rose, from its rose-red compounds","density":12.4,"meltingPoint":2237,"boilingPoint":3968,"halfLife":null},"group":{"n":9,"label":"Group 9","description":"Cobalt, rhodium, iridium, and meitnerium form group 9. These metals are prized as catalysts and for their exceptional hardness and corrosion resistance.","elements":["Co","Rh","Ir","Mt"]},"anomalies":[{"slug":"electron-config-anomalies","label":"Electron configu